TI  - Emotion Regulation and Resilience as Predictors of Volunteer Motivation Among Psychology Students: A Proposed Mental Health and Psychosocial Support Protocol
JO  - Psychology and Education A Multidisciplinary Journal
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.70838/pemj.650101
AB  - In a nation constantly tested by disasters, the Philippines faces relentless threats that demand stronger resilience and united action. Given that psychology students possess theoretical knowledge of helping behaviors, they often have limited structured opportunities to apply emotional self-regulation and resilience in actual volunteering settings. The study investigated the predictive roles of emotion regulation and resilience in volunteer motivation among psychology students in selected universities in Metro Cebu. Employing a descriptive predictive research design, the study utilized cluster sampling across the 3 selected universities, yielding a total of 830 participants. The study employed three standardized self‑report instruments: the Emotion Regulation Questionnaire (Gross & John, 2003), the Brief Resilience Scale (Smith et al., 2008), and the Volunteer Motivation Scale – Chinese Version (Li et al., 2016). Statistical analyses included descriptive statistics and ordinal logistic regression. Findings revealed that respondents demonstrated high levels of emotion regulation, moderate resilience, and average volunteer motivation. Regression results further established emotion regulation as a significant predictor (β = .364, p < .001). Meanwhile, resilience was found to be a non-significant predictor of volunteer motivation (β = −.011, ns, p = .755) The study suggests psychology volunteers with high emotional regulation are far more likely to sustain high motivation, making them valuable assets in volunteer initiatives. In contrast, volunteers with poor emotional regulation may struggle to stay motivated, requiring additional support or interventions. Based on the findings, the study proposes a mental health and psychosocial support protocol. It integrates emotion regulation strategies into volunteer preparedness and engagement, which may enhance cooperative capacity in Filipino communities.
ER  - 

