AB  - MOTIVATION FOR VOLUNTEERING ACROSS LIFE STAGES AMONG VOLUNTEERS IN NON-PROFIT ORGANIZATIONS T. Yamashita 1 , J. Keene 1 , C. Lu 1 , D. Carr 2 , 1. Sociology, University of Nevada Las Vegas, Las Vegas, Nevada, 2. Stanford University, Stanford, California Volunteering has been increasingly recognized as beneficial behaviors not only for individuals’ well-being but also for society’s. However, only a small fraction of US citizens regularly engage in volunteer activities. This study examined motivation to volunteer, and how these motivations are associated with actual interest in volunteering for individuals in three major life phases: early, middle, and later adulthood. Data were collected from1,046 adults who volunteered through at least one non-profit organization in Nevada, USA. Exploratory factor analysis examined the underlying motivations for each life stage. Although four motivation factors were identified, the underlying structure was not consistent across life stages. Community service, career advancement, and well-being were common motivations overall, however generativity among the later-adulthood group, and social network among the early- and middle-adulthood groups were unique motivations for volunteering. These motivational factors do not always translate to interest in volunteer activities. As such, this study further investigated the association between participants’ identified motivations and their actual interest in volunteering using proportional odds ordinal logistic regression. Results showed that the community service motivation was significantly associated with interest in volunteering among all life stages (Odds-ratios = 1.94 – 2.12, p < 0.05). Simultaneously, for the later-adulthood group, their interest in volunteering was motivated by a sense of generativity whereas for the early-adulthood group, career advancement was the main motivation for their interest in volunteer work. I this session, results and practical implications will be discussed in view of existing literature on volunteerism, the life course, and socioemotional selectivity theory.
