AU  - Mohd Zulkifli Abdullah*, Siti Noorsuriani Maon, Naliza Solat, Sarah Artiqah Mohamad Saad, Mas Anom Abdul Rashid
TI  - Assessing Sources of Stress towards the Mental Well-Being in Vaccine Administration Centres (VACs) Volunteers
JO  - Central European Management Journal
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.57030/3364890.cemj.31.1.62
AB  - COVID-19 has sparked a surge in volunteering in society. Volunteering offers a chance for people to support and contribute to society during the pandemic. It is crucial to study the impact of volunteering on mental health and well-being. Thus, this paper aims to investigate the influence of perceived stressors on mental well-being among VACs volunteers. An anonymous online survey was conducted among volunteers at VACs in Selangor regarding their perceived stress, mental health, and well-being symptoms during their initial volunteering months of the COVID-19 pandemic. Both descriptive statistics and multiple regression analyses were conducted. Participants were 226 volunteers (58% female; 42% male); nearly half were aged between 35 and 44 years (46.5%), 65% were from a non-academic background, and 60.6% were part-time non-clinical who completed the survey questionnaire. Regression results indicate that workload & environment and dealing with vaccine recipients significantly predict psychological distress, physical discomfort, and mental stress in volunteers during the current pandemic. Meanwhile, knowledge and skills are only significantly influenced by physical discomfort. The findings will assist policymakers and all stakeholders in managing the necessary measures to prevent mental health problems during the upcoming pandemic, guide future potential volunteering processes in emergencies and enhance community volunteering programs.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - P V Hayman
TI  - After the party: motivations for volunteer recruitment and retention in independent election campaigns
JO  - Parliamentary Affairs
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1093/pa/gsag030
AB  - Abstract Political participation is evolving rapidly, leading to consideration of whether motivations are likewise changing and how well widely accepted accounts of the incentives underpinning participation apply to new groups, such as independents. This study employs the General Incentives Model (GIM) to analyse the motivations for participation in the novel Australian independent campaigns of 2022. Data from 55 semi-structured interviews, supplemented by a survey of 282 highly engaged supporters, is analysed to develop a detailed account of the motivations underpinning involvement. The analysis identifies the presence of incentives from across the GIM and shows that incentives for recruitment and retention differed. Recruitment was primarily motivated by instrumental collective outcome incentives, while retention was largely underpinned by experiential selective process incentives. Interview results highlight how participant perceptions of the political context and volunteer roles shaped motivations. In addition to contributing new insights into motivations for non-party campaign participation, this study demonstrates the applicability of the GIM beyond party campaigns and indicates the importance of participant perceptions in shaping incentive salience.
