TY  - JOUR
AU  - S Lee
TI  - Volunteer-charity activity and its pathways to older adults’ social and emotional well-being
JO  - European Journal of Public Health
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.1093/eurpub/cky214.231
AB  - Background: Studies reveal that volunteering and civic activity participation play an important role in elderly’s physical and psychological well-being. The current study focuses on measures and correlates of older adults’ volunteer-charity activity, and how it is linked to their social and emotional well-being. Methods: We used data drawn from a sample of the Survey of Health, Ageing, and Retirement in Europe (SHARE) wave 6. A total sample of 21,257 male (45%) and 25,788 female (55%) aged 65+ from 18 European countries was analyzed. Study variables included frequency of volunteer-charity work, and social and emotional well-being indicators such as perceived health, psychological instability (i.e., loneliness and depression), positive affect and life satisfaction. The prevalence and correlates of volunteer-charity activity were examined using demographic and social variables. With weighted sample data, standardized estimates of path coefficients between the predictor variables were examined. Results: Although there was significant difference in the prevalence rate of volunteer-charity activity across EU societies, when controlling socio-demographic variables, those who participated in frequent volunteer-charity activities were more likely to report higher scores on self-rated health (p <.01), emotional stability (p <.001), and life satisfaction (p <.05). In addition, volunteer-charity activity was significantly and positively associated with optimism, meaning in life, and positive social support, which, in turn, affect psychological well-being among elderly persons. Conclusions: Emotional and social well-being is in part a byproduct of fulfilling experience through social and civic activities. Recommendation for culture and political initiatives should be included in public health agenda in order to rebuild different social roles and functions, and challenges for the older adults in a given society and culture. Key messages: Volunteer-charity activity in later life has a potential to elderly’s social-emotional thriving. Public health promotes cultural and political initiatives to facilitate older adults’ volunteering.
