AU  - Qi Zhi
AU  - Kristine Qureshi
TI  - Experiences and Psychosocial Impact of West Africa Ebola Deployment on US Health Care Volunteers
JO  - PLoS Currents
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1371/currents.outbreaks.c7afaae124e35d2da39ee7e07291b6b5
AB  - BACKGROUND: This qualitative study was designed to assess health care volunteers' experiences and psychosocial impacts associated with deployment to the West Africa Ebola epidemic. METHODS: In 2015, using snowball sampling, 16 US health care volunteers who had recently returned from West Africa were recruited for this study. Semi-structured interviews were conducted to collect information associated with each phase of deployment (pre, peri, and post). RESULTS: Participants reported that they were motivated to volunteer because of a sense of responsibility and feelings of empathy and altruism. Immediately prior to deployment, most reported fear of contagion and death, as well as doubts regarding the adequacy of their training. Family members and close friends expressed high levels of concern regarding participants' decisions to volunteer. During the deployment, participants were fearful of exposure and reported feeling emotionally and physically exhausted. They also reported feeling frustrated by extreme resource limitations, poor management of the mission, lack of clearly defined roles and responsibilities, and inability to provide high quality care. Upon return home, participants felt a sense of isolation, depression, stigmatization, interpersonal difficulties, and extreme stress. CONCLUSION: Preparedness of volunteers was suboptimal at each stage of deployment. All stakeholders, including volunteers, sponsoring organizations, government agencies, and professional organizations have a shared responsibility in ensuring that volunteers to medical missions are adequately prepared. This is especially critical for high risk deployments. Effective policies and practices need to be developed and implemented in order to protect the health and well-being of health care volunteers to the fullest extent possible.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - OLDER LIFELONG LEARNERS’ MOTIVATIONS FOR PARTICIPATING IN VOLUNTEER ACTIVITIES
JO  - The Gerontologist
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1093/geront/gnw162.1485
AB  - OLDER LIFELONG LEARNERS’ MOTIVATIONS FOR PARTICIPATING IN VOLUNTEER ACTIVITIES T. Yamashita, E. Lopez, J. Keene, M. Soligo, Sociology, University of Nevada Las Vegas, Las Vegas, Nevada Formal volunteering -- helping charitable organization(s) not for financial grain -- is associated with socioemotional gratification (e.g., generativity), personal development, and well-being in later life. As such, these factors should be primary motivations for volunteerism among older adults. Volunteer activities also provide older lifelong-learners unique opportunities for gaining new knowledge, skills, and social networks. Volunteering promotes quality of life as well as lifelong-learning above and beyond classroom settings. As such, older adults in general, and older lifelong-learners in particular, could benefit tremendously from volunteering. However, little is known about volunteer motivations among older lifelong-learners. This study analyzed data from 375 older adults in an urban community-based lifelong-learning program. Exploratory factor analysis of 18 motivation items from the Stanford Center on Longevity Volunteer Survey revealed three underlying motivational factors: generativity, personal development, and well-being, which is consistent with the previous studies. Logistic regression was used to examine the relationships between volunteer status and three identified motivational factors. Results showed that generativity (odds-ratio = 1.49, p < 0.05) was associated with volunteer activities even after adjusting for sociodemographic and health characteristics. Although lifelong-learners engagement in formal volunteer activities was motivated by a sense of generativity, both volunteers and non-volunteers may be overlooking opportunities for enhancing their personal development and well-being through volunteering. Based on these results, lifelong-learning programs may benefit from incorporating volunteer activities. Indeed, the synergistic benefits of combining lifelong-learning with volunteering for older community members may help recruit and retain their members. Detailed explanations and other suggestions are evaluated.
ER  - 

