TI  - Engaging Volunteers through Digital Platforms: Community, Motivation, and Inclusivity
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.64357/neya-gjnps-en-vo-di-2025
AB  - Volunteer engagement is a central pillar of effective non-profit management, influencing retention, satisfaction, and organizational impact. Digital platforms have expanded the opportunities for meaningful engagement by enabling interaction, recognition, and community-building across geographic boundaries. This article analyzes the strategies and tools that strengthen volunteer engagement online, with a focus on creating inclusive and motivating environments. It examines key approaches such as gamification, recognition systems, online community spaces, and real-time communication platforms. Case illustrations—including Crisis Text Line, Team Rubicon, and Kiva—demonstrate how digital tools enhance connection and sustain commitment. Attention is also given to inclusive practices that support low-skilled or low-digital literacy volunteers, such as simplified language, peer mentorship, and mobile-first platforms. The analysis highlights that volunteer engagement is not merely about assigning tasks but fostering identity, belonging, and purpose within digital communities. For practitioners, the findings provide actionable pathways to strengthen motivation and inclusivity; for scholars, they illuminate the intersections between technology, organizational behavior, and civic participation.
