TI  - An analysis of volunteers’ movement to serve, motivation and burnout working online at Fathers House Church Gqeberha
JO  - Nelson Mandela University Research Repository
PY  - 2022
AB  - The movement of volunteers to serve, the motivation behind serving and the effect of volunteering online has an impact on the volunteers at Fathers House Church in Gqeberha. Understanding the motivation of volunteers can lead to minimising the turnover of volunteers as well as increased satisfaction in volunteers which will allow leaders to prevent burnout of volunteers. Leaders need to understand how the COVID- 19 pandemic has affected volunteering and how willing or motivated volunteers are to volunteer virtually. Prior to 2019, before the COVID-19 pandemic, there was limited research done on virtual volunteering because volunteering was done in-person. Since 2019, the latest research show that there is an increased need for communication using technology, while there used to be a low demand for virtual volunteering due to individuals being able to volunteer in-person. Many businesses allow for in-person, as well as virtual volunteering. The main reason for this research is to determine the motivating factors as well as the level of involvement of the volunteers in Fathers House Church, a faith-based organisation in Gqeberha. This is important to determine whether there are different factors motivating volunteers and whether there is a difference in the styles and transitions of volunteering. The factors of burnout which occur in the life cycle of a volunteer are determined as well as the influential factors that lead to volunteers serving online. Finally, recommendations are made to improve and foster volunteerism at Fathers House Church. The research design is a positivist paradigm which is used where a scientific approach to research is adopted. The chosen paradigm for this research is a positivist paradigm, which adopts a scientific approach to research being done and seeks to enhance the relationship amongst variables as well as the knowledge surrounding a specific set of parameters. A sample of 42 respondents completed the survey where further information will be presented in chapter three. The most important conclusion in terms of volunteerism was that respondents place the greatest value on their capacity to assist others in their respective divisions. To achieve the maximum degree of happiness and fulfillment, helping others is believed to be of the iii utmost significance for volunteers and should be ingrained in the culture of each department and team leader. To maintain overall satisfaction and longevity for volunteers, the study shows that a team leader and management team must promote healthy attitudes and practices among volunteers serving within the church. iv ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS • To my Heavenly Father who never left me nor has He forsaken me. I am eternally grateful! • To my family, my wife Bianca for bringing motivation on the tough days and helping me every step of the way. I am truly blessed! • To my mother, for being a pillar of support and giving me the confidence to continue throughout the journey. • To my friend, Storm, who offered his support and encouragement through the study. • To Professor Paul Poisat for his guidance and encouragement. • To my MBA group members, Francis, Athi, Ella, Ryan and Sisanda, who made this journey enjoyable and mostly memorable. The MBA group has affected my life in such a positive manner and I am so thankful. • To the respondents who completed the questionnaire and took part in the study, thank you. • Lastly, my employer, Fathers House Church for giving me an opportunity, supporting me in my studies and allowing me to collect data from the volunteers. v
