AU  - AF Bo
AU  - BK Nielsen
AU  - GS Severinsen
AU  - ML Lasgaard
TI  - Volunteer-led intervention for loneliness and mental health in young adults: one-year follow-up
JO  - European Journal of Public Health
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1093/eurpub/ckag169.1986
AB  - Abstract Background Loneliness and poor mental health among young adults are major public health concerns. Civil society organisations increasingly contribute to addressing these challenges. This study evaluates one-year outcomes of a Danish volunteer-led intervention implemented across eight cities, offering social activities, group-based support and one-to-one mentoring for young adults aged 16–30. Methods A non-controlled before–after study used survey data collected at baseline (May 2022–September 2024) and one-year follow-up (May 2023–September 2025). Participants aged 16–30 were recruited at programme entry. Primary outcomes were loneliness (UCLA-12), depressive symptoms (MDI), and social anxiety (SIAS-6). Secondary outcomes included social support and social isolation. Changes were analysed using paired t-tests and McNemar’s test. Results Of 336 eligible participants, 164 completed follow-up (49%). Up to 118 participants were included in longitudinal analyses. No baseline differences were observed between responders and non-responders. Significant improvements were found in loneliness (−2.6, 95% CI − 3.5 to − 1.6, p < 0.001), depressive symptoms (−5.2, −7.1 to − 3.3, p < 0.001), and social anxiety (−1.2, −1.9 to − 0.4, p = 0.002). Social support increased modestly (p = 0.04), while no significant change was observed for social isolation. Follow-up participation increased over the study period after the introduction of retention strategies, including participant incentives and targeted communication. Conclusions Improvements in loneliness and mental health were observed among participants over one year. Findings should be interpreted with caution due to loss to follow-up and non-controlled design. The study highlights the importance of active retention strategies when evaluating community-based interventions in real-world settings. Key messages • Improvements in loneliness, depressive symptoms, social anxiety were observed among young adults in a volunteer-led intervention offering social activities, groups and mentoring in five Danish cities. • Evidence from civil society interventions is essential to inform public health strategies and strengthen responses to loneliness and mental health challenges.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Onur Sapci
AU  - Aliaksandr A. Amialchuk
AU  - Jon D. Elhai
TI  - Volunteering in Environmental Organizations and Subjective Well-Being: Evidence from a Nationally Representative, Longitudinal Dataset in the US
JO  - World
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.3390/world6030094
AB  - This study uses a nationally representative longitudinal dataset in the US to examine the long-term association of volunteering for environmental, recycling, and conservation groups with a person’s (a) willingness to continue to volunteer later in life and (b) several measures of their mental and physical well-being including perceived social status, optimism, psychological stress, suicidal thoughts and attempts, depressive symptoms and general self-reported physical health. By using Add Health data, we match responses to an environmental volunteerism question in Wave III (2002) with subjective well-being responses in Wave V (2016–2018) to examine the long-term association between these variables. After excluding missing responses, the analysis sample consists of 9800 individuals. After using linear survey regression analyses and several techniques based on propensity scores (stratification, weighting, matching) two key results emerged: first, being involved in environmental groups and organizations early in life showed a significant positive association with more hours spent on volunteering or community service work later in life; and second, people who volunteer in early adulthood are more optimistic, more sociable, have a higher perceived social status, display less stress and depressive symptoms.
