TY  - JOUR
AU  - O. V. Kopylova
AU  - Yu.S. Rakovskaya
AU  - Maria S. Yakunchikova
AU  - PO Savchuk
TI  - Medical Volunteers and their role in CVD prevention
JO  - European Heart Journal
PY  - 2020
DO  - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehaa1013
AB  - A volunteer organization formed 8 years ago in Russia has been working to promote awareness of cardiovascular diseases in the population. Education begins with children and continues into adulthood Prevention and timely detection of cardiovascular disease (CVD) is an important but sometimes difficult task. A new interesting and effective approach to solve this problem is to involve volunteers in CVD prevention activities. A special volunteer movement was organized in Russia in 2013 with the support of the government. The purpose was to involve young people, mainly students of medicine and other universities, in volunteer activities in the framework of preventive and medical assistance to the population. Later, this movement was named ‘Medical Volunteers’ (website: volmedic.com). Medical Volunteers provide significant support to the healthcare sector in solving socially significant problems in such priority areas as: Popularization of healthy lifestyles, education in prevention for the population Providing volunteer help to medical organizations First aid training and assistance during large-scale events Career guidance of schoolchildren of medical professions Popularization of blood donation Psychological service Special projects The main motive of the volunteers is to help the lay public and to increase their knowledge of medicine. Volunteering provides opportunities for the personal development of each participant, especially for volunteers in the health sector. Many of them are going to specialize in this field and volunteering allows them to gain much useful and interesting experience for their future profession even from school time. Future specialists form their skills and competencies, interact with the expert community: they learn how to perform various manipulations, develop skills for communication with patients, visit various hospital departments as volunteers, have the opportunity to determine their future specialty, and even find a job. A number of motivational events for volunteers are held annually: training courses, regional, and all-Russian forums of medical volunteers. Each region also has its own motivational components (e.g. incentive wrap-ups at the end of the calendar/school year). Moreover, volunteer activity is recognized and increases an applicant's opportunity to enter a chosen residency specialty. Usually, all volunteer activities in the movement are during a person’s leisure time. In 2020, in a number of regions, volunteering in the early university years was integrated into the educational syllabus. Award ceremony for best Medical Volunteers, International Volunteer Forum 2018 Award ceremony for best Medical Volunteers, International Volunteer Forum 2018 Medical Volunteers carry out systematic education on the prevention of CVD in schools and universities as well as in the workplaces. Based on the National Medical Research Centre for Therapy and Preventive Medicine of the Ministry of Health of Russia, volunteers regularly undergo certified full-time and distance learning via >100 educational programs. During a year, more than 1000 medical volunteers graduate from the educational courses. Every year they conduct more than 4500 training activities on preventive practices. Individual information, material, methodological guidelines, and best practice have been developed for all events and activities. Implementing the ‘Protecting Hearts’ program is to increase a healthy life expectancy for the population and reduce mortality from CVD and its complications. As part of the program, volunteers conduct systematic activities with the population of different ages by organizing interactive events. In 2019, the campaign reached over 1 million people and volunteers held over 800 events with the population. Due to the restrictions imposed by the Corona virus pandemic in 2020, more than 400 events had been organized in person and also online. Medical volunteers have been providing support to medical personnel in 560 hospitals and clinics for 7 years. 7400 volunteers carry out activities according to agreements with each medical organization. Between 2013 and 2019, our volunteers assisted in different hospitals for more than 2.5 million hours. There are special projects conducted by volunteers. One such is the Federal program ‘Children for Adults’ Protection’. The aim is to increase the level of awareness in schoolchildren about strokes, as well as to develop their skills in recognizing the symptoms. 30 000 pupils from 80 Russian cities took part in this program. One important task for the volunteers is first aid training and assistance during large-scale events. A unique educational course and the methodological manual have been developed for this purpose. Since the beginning, with the participation of 5500 trained medical volunteers, more than 1915 sporting and public events have been held, the largest of which included the Moscow Marathon and the 2018 FIFA World Cup. Training of first aid skills for children by Medical Volunteers Training of first aid skills for children by Medical Volunteers The all-Russian special project #ДоброВСело (KindnessToTheVillage) is being actively implemented. The project has become one of the most successful volunteer initiatives launched in 2018 as a part of the Year of the Volunteer. Medical volunteers in 85 regions of Russia have helped 1307 paramedic-midwife organizations, visited 2507 remote settlements to increase local preventive knowledge, and provide medical care to the rural population. Medical volunteers are working all around Russia to build people's commitment to a healthy lifestyle. Students at physical education universities, pedagogy and psychology, current athletes and trainers, as well as any interested students of non-medical specialties and organizations are involved. The interactive formats are used in this work: all-Russian and inter-regional actions, master classes, lectures, debates, visual experiments, etc. On 21 March 2020, the all-Russian Mutual Aid Action #МыВместе (WeAreTogether) was launched—it united more than 118 000 people in 85 regions who decided to help others during the pandemic. Volunteers provide assistance in the delivery of food, medicine, and psychological wellbeing to the elderly and people with disabilities, large families, and residents in remote locations. Additionally, more than 7000 volunteers are assisting in 288 medical organizations located in 58 regions of the country. Depending on the level of training, volunteers are involved in administrative work or in patient care. Volunteers help with transportation, feeding, sanitation, temperature, blood pressure, and pulse measurements, dressings, collection and delivery of tests. At the same time, safety measures are strictly observed. Medical organizations set specific requirements for medical examinations and training of volunteers. Medical volunteering is a unique opportunity for the prevention of CVD and other conditions. Medical Volunteers learn by themselves and teach others. The desire to help people is the main driving force of the movement. Such motivation combined with professional and creative approaches ensures effectiveness and long-term benefit for society. The data underlying this article will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author. Conflict of interest: none declared.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Tetyana Blyznyuk
AU  - Marharyta Sobakar
TI  - Risks in Volunteer Activities during the War in Ukraine
JO  - Ukrainian Journal of Applied Economics and Technology
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.36887/2415-8453-2024-2-6
AB  - The significant role of volunteer activities in social development and solving socially significant problems is recognized internationally and nationally. Now, volunteerism serves as an expression of civic maturity, contributing to stabilizing and regulating social relations. In the context of a full-scale war in Ukraine, the role of volunteering in providing the army with essential support is continuously increasing. However, volunteer activities are characterized by a high degree of risk in such circumstances. The article aims to analyze the risks of volunteer activities during the war in Ukraine. The article explores the fundamental features and directions of volunteer activities in Ukraine, including support for the army, assistance to internally displaced persons and refugees, and other relevant social issues. It identifies the transformation of the volunteer environment in the country from chaotic to structured and professional since the onset of the full-scale invasion. The article also highlights the challenges faced by volunteer organizations, including reduced aid volumes, volunteer safety issues, lack of coordination and effective control over donated property, and others. The paper analyzes the principal negative aspects of volunteer activities that may pose risks, including the expenditure of internal resources, globalization and hyperbolization of activities, elevated demands on the surrounding community, overestimation of one's significance, savior complex, object of aid incapacitation, and authoritarianism. It is noted that volunteerism can be particularly hazardous in terms of emotional burnout since volunteers need to provide not only their time and resources but also emotional support. Risks associated with postponing the right to enjoy life and immersing the volunteer in the role of a "savior" that can lead to alienation from others are also considered. In this regard, volunteerism may become a source of conflict. The article suggests ways to address the risks and negative aspects of volunteer activities in Ukraine's war conditions. Emphasis is placed on controlling emotions, motivation, and relationships; monitoring mental health; comparing assigned tasks with personal resources; realistically understanding one's capabilities; and not postponing essential moments. Keywords: volunteering, exhaustion, emotional burnout, savior complex, psyche, problems, risks.
ER  - 

