AU  - Khrystyna S. Stoliaruk
AU  - Mariia V. Fedorkovych
TI  - Volunteering As a Trajectory of Professional Development in Social Entrepreneurship
JO  - Business Inform
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.32983/2222-4459-2026-2-398-406
AB  - The article focuses on analyzing volunteering as an activity that requires the development of specific competencies and the potential to evolve into a new professional path in the labor market. It has been found that volunteering involves a range of functions that develop certain knowledge and skills in a person, distinguishing a true volunteer from someone engaged in pseudo-volunteering. To reinforce this trend in practice, a study was conducted on the role of higher education institutions, which have integrated courses dedicated to developing volunteering competencies into their educational and professional programs. A theoretical analysis of perspectives and approaches regarding the meaning and content of volunteer activities was conducted, leading to the conclusion that such activities can serve as a strong springboard for career development in the field of social entrepreneurship. The latter involves expanding the competencies that a volunteer should possess and developing new ones within higher education to meet the requirements of effective social management. An analysis was carried out of educational and professional programs at higher education institutions that integrated specializations and specific courses directly aimed at forming and developing the competencies of a social entrepreneur. Based on the study’s results, it is proposed to consider three key components of a social enterprise’s human resource potential: educational – ensures the development of basic and specialized competencies necessary for creating and launching social enterprises; career – guarantees the stability of the professional path, opportunities for vertical and horizontal mobility, as well as the capacity for continuous innovation; motivational – enhances internal and external engagement in social projects and supports long-term commitment to the social mission. It has been found that volunteer experience promotes the development of entrepreneurial identity, strengthens motivation to create sustainable social models, and develops a unique competency profile that combines social sensitivity, managerial skills, and the ability to innovate in the field of public good, making volunteers attractive candidates for the roles of founders or key managers of social enterprises.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - William J. Rothwell
TI  - AI-Augmented Succession Planning in Volunteer Organizations
JO  - International Journal of AI in Pedagogy Innovation and Learning Futures
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.46787/ijaipil.v1i2.8058
AB  - Volunteer organizations play a critical role in civil society, yet many face increasing difficulty finding, developing, and replacing volunteer leaders. Demographic change, evolving patterns of civic participation, competing demands on volunteers’ time, and volunteer fatigue create significant challenges for leadership continuity. Although succession planning has been extensively studied in corporate and professional contexts, less attention has been given to volunteer organizations, where authority, engagement, and leadership development operate differently.This article examines succession challenges in volunteer organizations and argues that succession planning should be understood as an ongoing responsibility of governance and stewardship rather than a response to leadership vacancies. Drawing on literature concerning succession planning, nonprofit leadership, volunteer management, social capital, and civic engagement, the article proposes an actionable six-step model emphasizing leadership commitment, role clarity, succession-risk assessment, identification and development of potential successors, and redesign of leadership roles to strengthen continuity and leadership pipelines. A hypothetical community arts organization illustrates the model’s practical application.The article further examines how artificial intelligence can augment succession planning in resource-constrained volunteer organizations through knowledge capture, role clarification, leadership development, and continuity planning. AI is conceptualized as a capacity-building tool rather than a substitute for human judgment. Volunteer leaders remain responsible for organizational context, confidentiality, fairness, governance, and succession decisions. The resulting approach positions AI-augmented succession planning as a human-centered strategy for strengthening leadership continuity, institutional knowledge, and organizational resilience.
ER  - 

