TY  - JOUR
AU  - Andreea CORSEI
TI  - VOLUNTEERING AS A FORM OF SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY AND CIVIC INVOLVEMENT: LEGAL AND ETHICAL DIMENSIONS
JO  - Valahia University Law Study
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.53373/vuls.2025.45.1.060
AB  - Volunteering is an essential expression of active citizenship and social responsibility in a state governed by the rule of law.From a legal perspective, it is regulated in Romania by Law no.78/2014, which establishes the legal framework for carrying out volunteering activities, establishing the rights and obligations of volunteers, as well as the role of host organizations.The article analyzes the legal dimension of volunteering in relation to labor law, the recognition of professional experience acquired and the compatibility with European regulations on the matter.In parallel, the ethical implications of volunteering are highlighted, seen not only as an unpaid activity, but as a moral manifestation of solidarity, altruism and commitment to the common good.The risks of instrumentalizing the volunteer, the importance of transparency in the contractual relationship and the need for social recognition of volunteering are addressed.The paper emphasizes the strategic role of volunteering in strengthening social cohesion, in promoting democratic values and in the community response to contemporary challenges.Directions are proposed for legislative harmonization, professionalization and ethical valorization of volunteering activity, in accordance with European principles and the standards of an inclusive and participatory society.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Sunday Hull
TI  - Examining Social and Intrapersonal Factors Among Youth Crisis Line Volunteers
JO  - Old Dominion University
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.25777/fgzn-1113
AB  - Crisis lines provide a free crisis response service that is highly accessible to individuals who are at elevated risk of suicide and may not be otherwise connected with care. In order to best tailor crisis line interventions to youth populations, a number of youth-to-youth crisis lines invite youth callers to connect with trained youth volunteers. While prior research has examined adult crisis lines workers’ experience and well-being and identified key factors that predict positive and negative volunteering outcomes, far less is known about similar determinants of experience and well-being among adolescent volunteers on youth-to-youth crisis lines. The purpose of this study was to explore the longitudinal development of social factors (peer support and supervisory support) and intrapersonal factors (self-compassion and self-efficacy) among youth-to-youth crisis line volunteers over their first year of volunteering and to test the longitudinal impact of within-person fluctuation in these factors on both positive and negative experience and well-being outcomes. Results suggested that self-compassion and self-efficacy increased over youth’s first year of volunteering. These findings highlight the potential benefits for youth volunteering on youth-to-youth crisis lines. The social and intrapersonal factors were correlated with the majority of the experience and well-being outcomes (e.g., compassion satisfaction, burnout, secondary traumatic stress) in the expected directions at baseline. However, when the social and intrapersonal factors were explored as within-person predictors of these outcomes at the subsequent study time points (3 months intervals), no significant lagged effects were found. Significant associations between study variables at baseline suggest that these factors are relevant to volunteer experience and well-being, but the lack of significant lagged effects suggests that within-person fluctuations in these factors do not predict volunteer outcomes three months later. Future research is warranted to explore trajectories earlier in the crisis line experience, such as during training, and to explore lagged effects on different timescales, such as a weekly basis. Additional studies could also examine potential moderators of trajectories and lagged relationships. Further research is warranted to continue to explore the benefits and risks of youth crisis line volunteering and to identify factors that may impact youth volunteer experience and well-being.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Shahrizul Amizan Sahari
AU  - Kuldip Singh
AU  - Noni Harianti Junaidi
TI  - INTRINSIC MOTIVATION AND EXTRA-ROLE BEHAVIOR: EXAMINING THE IMPACT OF VOLUNTEERING MEANINGFULNESS ON ORGANIZATIONAL CITIZENSHIP AMONG SARAWAK CIVIL SERVANTS
JO  - International Journal of Law Government and Communication
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.35631/ijlgc.1041017
AB  - Recent reforms in Sarawak’s civil service have replaced pension benefits with a contribution-based scheme for new hires, raising concerns about equity, morale, and retention. This study explores whether Volunteering Meaningfulness (VM) influences Organizational Citizenship Behavior (OCB) among civil servants in this changing policy environment. Guided by equity theory and intrinsic motivation literature, survey data were collected and analyzed to examine the relationship between VM and OCB. The results show that employees who find volunteering meaningful are more likely to engage in positive, discretionary workplace behaviors. These findings suggest that promoting meaningful voluntary activities can help sustain motivation and commitment despite structural changes. The study contributes to public sector research by highlighting the role of intrinsic motivation in shaping workplace behavior.
