TI  - O2-5 Mobilising People as Assets for community-based active ageing promotion: A multi-stakeholder perspective on peer volunteering initiatives
JO  - European Journal of Public Health
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1093/eurpub/ckac094.013
AB  - Abstract Background Citizens who contribute as volunteers and peer mentors within a community are important assets that can be mobilised to improve health and wellbeing. In order to optimise the contribution of peer-volunteers to active ageing initiatives, we need to understand their experiences and identify ways to support them in preparing for the role and overcoming potential challenges. Aim: This study synthesises the perspectives of a range of stakeholders involved in peer volunteering active ageing initiatives implemented in UK and provides an in-depth account of how such initiatives can effectively mobilise individuals as community assets. It draws on the experience of all actors involved in such initiatives, ranging from volunteers, recipients of volunteering actions, volunteer managers and volunteering service providers. Methods This evidence synthesis used qualitative data from (a) three studies focusing on the development (phase 1), feasibility trial evaluation (phase 2) and community roll-out (phase 3) of ACE (Active, Connected, Engaged), a peer volunteering active ageing intervention, alongside (b) the experiences of third sector organisations in providing peer volunteering programmes. Ten managers, 22 volunteers and 20 ACE participants were interviewed. Interviews were audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim and analysed using framework analysis. Results Seven main themes including 33 higher and 22 lower order themes were identified: Motives; Benefits; Characteristics of peer volunteers; Challenges; Training needs; Recruitment; Successful strategies for maintenance. Altruism, changes in life circumstances, opportunities to reconnect with the community and personal fulfilment were the main reasons for volunteering. Volunteering was described as being personally rewarding, an avenue to acquire new skills and knowledge, and increased social connections and physical activity. Effective volunteers are committed, reliable, have a good sense of humour, good interpersonal skills and are able to relate to the participants. When pairing volunteers with participants, clarity of role, level of time commitment, shared interests and geographical proximity are worth considering. Conclusions Successful implementation of peer-volunteering initiatives requires the use of appropriate recruitment routes, easy joining procedures, provision of ongoing support, good communication across all stakeholders, feedback and recognition of effort and a dedicated team to build resilience and provide volunteers with administrative support.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Cicih Handika
AU  - Elok Savitri Pusparini
TI  - Volunteer Intention to Stay in Non-Governmental Organizations: Exploring the Role of Calling through the Lens of Work-as-Calling Theory to Strengthen the Economy
JO  - International Journal of Economics Development Research (IJEDR)
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.37385/ijedr.v6i1.6857
AB  - This study examines the relationship between perceiving a calling and intention to stay among volunteers of non-governmental organisations (NGOs) in Jabodetabek, with the mediating role of living a calling. Through a quantitative approach and the Covariance-Based Structural Equation Modelling (CBSEM) method, data from 350 respondents were analyzed. The results showed that perceiving a calling has a significant influence on intention to stay, and living a calling strengthens the relationship. Managerial implications include the importance of programs that align with volunteers' intrinsic motivation, recognition of contributions, training, and the creation of a supportive work environment. This study is limited to the Jabodetabek area with a small sample size. Further studies are expected to cover a wider area and use mixed methods to enrich the results. This study provides practical insights for NGOs to improve volunteer retention through a calling-based approach.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Wanpatsorn Thanabadeejindapat
AU  - Phra Medhivajarapundit
AU  - Phramaha Weerasak Abhinandavedi
TI  - A Mindfulness-Based Development Process for Volunteer Work in Buddhist Healthcare: A Case Study of Siriwattago Medical Clinic under the Buddhamahametta Foundation
JO  - The Journal of International Buddhist Studies College
PY  - 2025
AB  - This study investigates the challenges faced by volunteers at the Siriwattago Medical Clinic (Acupuncture), operating under the Buddhamahametta Foundation, and examines the development and impact of a contemplative volunteer training program centred on mindfulness and loving-kindness (mettā) meditation. The research aimed to (1) identify key problems and obstacles in volunteer work, (2) develop a structured process using Buddhist contemplative practices to enhance peaceful well-being, and (3) evaluate the program’s effectiveness in improving volunteer experience and group dynamics. Data were collected through in-depth interviews with 24 active volunteers, field observations during clinic operations and meditation sessions over four weeks, and post-program reflective feedback. The data were analysed using descriptive statistics and thematic content analysis. Findings revealed three main categories of challenges: (1) personal readiness and emotional well-being, including stress, fatigue, and lack of self-confidence; (2) interpersonal coordination and communication issues, such as misunderstandings, emotional reactivity, and weak collaborative engagement; and (3) insufficient operational training, with unclear role definitions and limited feedback systems. To address these, a three-phase volunteer development model was implemented: (1) awareness-building through mindfulness and loving-kindness workshops, (2) integration of contemplative practices into weekly volunteer routines, including guided meditation and group reflection, and (3) the establishment of communication guidelines rooted in compassion and respect. Post-intervention assessments indicated significant improvements in emotional regulation, interpersonal patience, and a strengthened sense of purpose among volunteers. Participants reported enhanced inner calm, empathy, and teamwork. The overall group dynamic shifted toward greater harmony and mutual support. This study concludes that the integration of Buddhist contemplative practices into volunteer development effectively fosters individual well-being and promotes a spiritually grounded, compassionate environment for service delivery.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Meagan Doran
AU  - Beth Hess
AU  - Keirsten Andersen
TI  - Rising to the Challenge: How Medical Reserve Corps Volunteers Serve as a Scalable Workforce for Local Health Departments in Response to COVID-19 and Beyond
JO  - Journal of Public Health Management and Practice
PY  - 2022
DO  - 10.1097/phh.0000000000001605
AB  - The COVID-19 pandemic presented local health departments (LHDs) with an unprecedented challenge—how to quickly meet the constantly shifting and diverse health and support needs of communities through a lengthy and evolving crisis. Early in the response, the need for a scalable emergency public health workforce to augment public health services became evident. The efficient and effective deployment of community volunteers was critical to the success of the pandemic migration strategy of local governments to address a variety of overwhelmed public services such as public information, health care support, food security services, infection prevention, and contact tracing. The Medical Reserve Corps (MRC), a national network of more than 200 000 medical and support volunteers, was a key resource for LHDs responding to the COVID-19 crisis throughout the United States.1 A Capable, Ready, and Responsive Workforce The MRC was created by congressional legislation in response to the need for a public health volunteer response mechanism following the terrorist events of September 11, 2001.1 Since 2002, local emergency response capacity has expanded greatly through the development of nearly 800 MRC units throughout the United States, whose volunteers are trained and capable to respond to the public health consequences of a variety of local, state, and regional emergencies.1 The 2020 Network Profile of the Medical Reserve Corps, which broadly surveyed unit leaders across all 10 US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) regions, found that, where implemented, MRC units have become an integral part of their communities' long-term emergency and public health plans.2 In fact, 85% of surveyed MRC units reported being with their housing organization (LHDs, state health departments, emergency management agencies, health systems, etc) for more than 5 years, and 91% of units stated that their volunteers were integrated as a valuable part of their housing organization's greater emergency management plans.2 Since its inception, the MRC network has responded to a range of emergencies, including the H1N1 outbreak, Zika, hurricanes, wildfires, and tornadoes. Unit volunteers also support the health, safety, and emergency preparedness of their communities, including search and rescue operations, first aid and medical support during large public gatherings, community education and outreach, behavioral health support, and veterinary care. None of these previous activities engaged, nor demonstrated, the capabilities of the network as much as the COVID-19 pandemic. MRC Answers the Call in Unprecedented Times In the 2020 Network Profile of the Medical Reserve Corps, 84% of MRC units reported supporting COVID-19 response efforts.2 As the Figure shows, from January 1 to December 31, 2020, MRC volunteers contributed to a variety of local COVID-19 response efforts to total approximately 840 000 hours including approximately 330 000 hours to testing, 86 000 hours to call centers, and 142 000 hours to contact tracing.2,3 As LHDs prepared for the delivery of COVID-19 vaccines, 89% of MRC units reported being prepared to support mass vaccinations through Point of Dispensing events, with 82% of units providing community public health emergency preparedness and response trainings to their volunteers.2 Based on the 2021 volunteer value developed by the Independent Sector, MRC volunteers are estimated to have saved local health jurisdictions an estimated $85 million through the provision of both medical and support services from an estimated 3 million hours of donated volunteer service in response to the COVID-19 pandemic.3,4FIGURE: MRC Unit Activities in Response to the COVID-19 PandemicAbbreviations: MRC, Medical Reserve Corps; PPE, personal protective equipment. aReprinted with permission from National Association of County and City Health Officials.2 This figure is available in color online (www.JPHMP.com).“The MRC network has played and will continue to play a substantial role at every stage of the current response to the COVID-19 pandemic,” said Dawn O'Connell, HHS Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response. “Without a doubt, unit volunteers and leaders saved lives with their contributions to testing, contact tracing, call center support, vaccinations, and other response activities.”2 A few examples of MRC units at work in their communities during the pandemic are as follows: The Norfolk MRC in Virginia provided support in a variety of capacities including staffing public information call centers and COVID-19 testing and vaccination events as well as conducting infection prevention activities such as personal protective equipment (PPE) fit testing.5 Among their community partnerships, the unit worked with Norfolk Public Schools to assist with contact tracing, creation of mitigation teams, developing return-to-school protocols, and providing local elementary school students aged 5 to 11 years with first and second doses of COVID-19 vaccines.5 The Oklahoma City MRC in Oklahoma developed Strike Teams, which were deployed to offer vaccination, translation, and health education in partnership with community-based groups including Latinx churches, the Oklahoma City Asian Coalition, the Islamic Society of Oklahoma City, and a Hindu temple to ensure equitable access to vaccinations.6 To help combat social isolation as exacerbated by the COVID-19 pandemic, on Vashon Island in Washington State, MRC volunteers staffed a Community Care Team Help Line for 12 hours each day, 7 days per week. Volunteers contributed more than 700 hours per month to provide mental health and spiritual support to members of their community.7 Strengthening the Capabilities of the MRC Network As communities look to build resilience through the creation or strengthening of MRC units locally, a variety of resources are available. The HHS Office of the Assistant Secretary of Preparedness and Response (ASPR) MRC Program Office provides a wealth of materials and trainings to help new or existing units thrive, including a network of Regional Liaisons who provide one-on-one technical assistance to local MRC leaders. The National Association of County and City Health Officials (NACCHO) works to support the nationwide MRC network through a cooperative agreement with ASPR to develop resources needed to build a competent and successful emergency public health volunteer workforce. For example, in the 2020 project year, NACCHO awarded $1.1 million through the competitive Operational Readiness Awards to 202 MRC units across the country to build their volunteer and unit response capabilities.8 On average, for every dollar awarded, MRC units and their communities saw $6.85 returned through award activities.8 As the pandemic evolved, MRC units nimbly adapted their award and unit activities to augment the public health workforce through contact tracing, distributing PPE, establishing and staffing call centers/help lines, supporting COVID-19 screening and testing, providing disaster behavioral and mental health support, and conducting community outreach. In fact, nearly 15 000 MRC volunteers served more than 369 000 hours on the implementation of 2020 Operational Readiness Award activities alone.8 Moreover, the 2020 Network Profile of the Medical Reserve Corps found an increased reliance by MRC units on Operational Readiness Award funding in 2020 compared with 2017, with 45% of units reporting that NACCHO awards account for as much as 75% to 100% of their full operating budgets.8 In addition to critical funding, NACCHO serves as a conduit in the exchange of resources, best practices, and lessons learned across the MRC network through ongoing e-mail newsletters, reporting, and resource collection on the NACCHO Web site. For example, NACCHO provides MRC units with a library of deployment readiness resources, most notably, the MRC Deployment Readiness Guide. First published in 2019, the MRC Deployment Readiness Guide supports the integration and deployment of the MRC in local and multijurisdictional medical/public health emergency responses and provides tools, resources, and recommendations to meet national standards for volunteer deployment readiness. In addition to defining national standards for volunteer management, the resources included in the guide help develop volunteer and unit capabilities that can be modified to fit the unique mission of individual MRC units. Investing in Community Preparedness The integration and expansion of the MRC network throughout the nation have consistently proven to be effective in fostering the growth, resilience, and safety of the communities where they are embraced. In addition, MRC utilization has directly translated to impressive workforce savings and remarkable returns on investment. The expanded utilization of MRC units at the local, state, and federal levels through funding and resource partnership with NACCHO is expected to lead to even greater public health and emergency response outcomes into the future. By building upon past successes, communities that prioritize the MRC in their emergency public health planning are ensuring that their jurisdictions build a sustainable, trained, and ready workforce capable of surging to meet any challenges while ensuring the resiliency of their communities.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Ahmed Mostafa Rady
AU  - Tarek Moloukhiya
AU  - Osama Elkholy
AU  - Enas Shawky
TI  - Getting Involved In Volunteer Work Could Protect Against Relapse among Detoxified Opiate Patients: A Six Months Follow Up Study
JO  - Neuropsychiatry
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.4172/neuropsychiatry.1000458
AB  - Background and objectives Drug abuse is a major mental health concern. Though diverse psychological interventions are available such as cognitive behavioral, psychodynamic, hypnotherapy, group, family, community based and Motivation enhancement psychotherapy as well as 12 step model of Narcotics Anonym NA, but still high relapse rates are reported in literature. Our study aims at assessing an innovative approach based on shifting focus of attention from self to overcome obsessive self-concern, which can be partly categorized under community based interventions Methods 60 opiate addict patients were recruited within one week after hospitalized detoxification at Mamoura Psychiatric Hospital. Active phase (3 months): 30 patients went through two hours structured cognitive behavioral CBT group therapy (max. 10 patients in a group) twice weekly and the other 30 patients went through shifting focus approach by implicating them in volunteer act at Alexandria University Hospital Hepatology department for 2 hours volunteer work twice weekly with a weekly group meeting (max. 10 patients in a group) with psychotherapist, who carried out group animation focusing on experience exchange in a supportive encouraging relational frame. Follow up phase (3 months): both groups were seen on monthly basis as booster session keeping the same psychotherapeutic modality frame. Both active and follow up phases were carried out on outpatient basis. Abstinence was assessed by urine screening on monthly bases and quality of life was psychometrically assessed Results Both groups were balanced in terms of age, marital status, age at onset of drug abuse and base line score of quality of life scale QLS. After 6 months, no difference was found between both groups in terms of abstinence rate, QLS score and increment of QLS score (p>0.05) Conclusion Shifting focus approach by implicating detoxified patients in regular volunteer act reveals as efficacious as structured group CBT approach in a non-inferiority study design
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Diane Crone
AU  - Faaiza Bashir
AU  - Rachel Clair Sumner
AU  - Zsofia Szekeres
TI  - 72 The role of conservation projects for health enhancing physical activity (HEPA): holistic benefits for volunteers whilst enhancing environmental biodiversity - a blue space case study
JO  - European Journal of Public Health
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1093/eurpub/ckae114.190
AB  - Abstract Purpose This qualitative study explored how engagement activities at a community reservoir in Cardiff, Wales supported the health and wellbeing of the local community, improved access to green and blue spaces, and enhanced the biodiversity of the environment. There were two specific aims; to capture the lived experiences of volunteers involved in the preservation of the reservoir for over 15 years, and; to understand the experiences, motivations, and outcomes of volunteers taking part in community-based conservation activities at the site from 2021. The research is innovative because it captured the experiences of participants (volunteers) who had been involved in the ‘saving of the site’, and volunteers involved in enhancing the site as a community asset during the last 4 years. It therefore captures experiences from nearly two decades of voluntary action in local nature conservation from volunteering perspectives. Methods Data were collected through interviews and focus groups from a total of 12 participants (six participants involved in the preservation of the site, and six from the current volunteer base). Data were collected during the summer of 2023 and analysed using thematic analysis (Braun and Clarke, 2019). Analysis provided key findings (themes) linked to the aims detailed above. Results Findings from the two sets of volunteers highlighted the following themes: (i) value of nature for health, wellbeing and spirituality; (ii) motivation to taking part in volunteering to support the environment and for self-confidence and a sense of purpose; (iii) health enhancing physical activity opportunities; and (iv) the provision for getting outdoors for activity, fresh air and tranquillity. Conclusions Findings highlight the potential of such projects for health enhancing physical activity and the importance of them for holistic mental, social and environmental health benefits for both individuals, and the physical environment. Findings confirm the many ecological, health and wellbeing benefits for both volunteers and the environment from conservation community projects. The holistic benefits of such programmes should be acknowledged, highlighted, and promoted to support both volunteers’ health and to support the current environmental challenges faced in local communities.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
AU  - Sampada Kumar Dash
AU  - Pinaki Das Gupta
AU  - P. V. K. Sasidhar
TI  - Social Partnership With Multinational Enterprises (MNEs), and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) in Post-COVID Times in an Emerging Economy Like India – Integrating Volunteering and Social Entrepreneurship as Newer Strategic Healthcare Model t
JO  - Journal of Strategic Innovation and Sustainability
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.33423/jsis.v20i1.7544
AB  - The COVID-19 pandemic exposed critical weaknesses in economic and healthcare systems, affecting India due to structural inequities and limited healthcare infrastructure. This study examines the role of Multinational Enterprises (MNEs) and Micro, Small, and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) in strengthening healthcare systems through volunteering and social entrepreneurship, contributing to Sustainable Development Goal 3 (SDG-3): ensuring healthy lives and well-being for all. Adopting a mixed-methods approach, the research integrates systematic secondary analysis with primary data collection from 150 structured surveys and 25 in-depth interviews with healthcare and MSME stakeholders. Findings indicate that MSMEs played a critical role in maintaining healthcare supply chains, while volunteer-driven engagement models helped counter misinformation and enhance community resilience. This study advances the understanding of MSME-driven healthcare resilience during crises, filling a gap in research on corporate social entrepreneurship and public-private synergies in pandemic response. It underscores the need for structured public-private partnerships, institutionalized social entrepreneurship, and expanded digital health integration to strengthen pandemic resilience.
ER  - 
